{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear drift toward a static mean (approx. 81-82 EUR), failing to capture the sharp volatility and subsequent crash to 75-77 EUR observed in the true data. Apply a downward correction of 3-5 EUR for horizons beyond 10 days when the recent trend shows a peak followed by a decline.",
    "Address lag in trend reversal: The model exhibits a significant lag in reacting to price drops. When the 18-day history shows a peak (e.g., >84 EUR) followed by a drop, the model continues to forecast an increase or stability for 5-7 days. Adjust forecasts downward immediately upon detecting a local maximum in the history window.",
    "Incorporate sentiment sensitivity: When sentiment history deviates from zero (e.g., 0.40 or 0.20), the model fails to adjust its trajectory. Treat non-zero sentiment values as a signal to override the model's mean-reverting assumption with a more aggressive bearish or bearish-consolidation bias, as positive sentiment in this dataset correlates with the tail end of a rally before a sharp fall.",
    "Reduce overshoot after spikes: The model's linear projection causes it to overshoot the true price during the initial phase of a decline (e.g., forecasting 81.60 when the true price drops to 80.62). Apply a dampening factor to the first 5 days of the forecast when the immediate history shows a downward slope.",
    "Horizon-specific volatility adjustment: The model underestimates the amplitude of price swings. For horizons 15-30 days, apply a wider confidence interval or a specific correction factor to account for the observed drop to 75 EUR, which the model completely misses."
  ]
}
